ttychatter.mailbot 0.6.0
=========================

ttychatter.mailbot is an IMAP/sendmail or IMAP/SMTP transport adapter for
ttychatter. It watches unread mail, maps accepted messages to either a
chat session or a mailbot control command, calls ttychatter.python when model
work is needed, and replies by email.

The mailbot is deliberately not an OpenRouter client. Model access, model-cache
updates, model lists, memory, saved sessions, attachments, and chat behavior are
still delegated to ttychatter.python.

Main features
-------------

    - Private mode with ALLOWED_FROM sender gating.
    - Public demo mode with --open-to-public or OPEN_TO_PUBLIC=1.
    - Self-sender rejection and DENY_FROM/BANNED_LIST for public safety.
    - Public senders can use ordinary email subjects as prompts; tc: is not
      required in public mode.
    - Public senders are isolated by PUBLIC_SESSION_MODE, default per-sender.
    - Public replies advertise the actual session name; a normal reply with
      Re: session-name can resume when RE_SESSION_SUBJECTS permits it.
    - Public sessions are automatically cleaned up by age, idle time, count,
      and optional transcript trimming.
    - Expiring public session logs can be emailed to their owner before local
      deletion. Public users can toggle this preference when enabled.
    - Per-sender user registry with private ttychatter HOME directories.
    - Subject-line sessions: ordinary subjects become session names, so Re:
      resumes the same sender/session pair.
    - Per-user ttychatter preferences such as MODEL and CONTEXT_TURNS by email.
    - Per-user OpenRouter API keys stored in chmod 600 secret files, not config.
    - Server fallback OpenRouter key loaded from a secret file.
    - Spam protection for auto-submitted/list/bulk mail, recipient floods, and
      sender/global rate limits.
    - Configurable mail grammar for the subject prefix, separator, session
      delimiters, body-command subjects, and subject/body command acceptance.
    - Uniform private command syntax:

          tc:CONFIG
          tc:MODELS
          tc:MODELS TEXT
          tc:CMD
          tc:command args
          tc:{session-name}
          tc:{new}

    - Subject: MAILBOT can contain either a command or an ordinary AI prompt in
      the message body. Known command lines run as commands; other body text is
      treated as a new chat request.
    - Config export/update by email through tc:CONFIG.
    - Cache-only model cache retrieval through tc:MODELS or tc:model-cache.
    - Cache-only model cache search through tc:MODELS TEXT or tc:model-search TEXT.
    - Explicit model-cache refresh through tc:update-models.
    - Configurable command output delivery: auto, body, or attachment.
    - Session listing with head/tail previews from each .log file.
    - Session search, rename, delete, and dry-run deletion.
    - Remote retrieval of the mailbot manpage.
    - Safe allowlisted ttychatter utility commands over email.

Files
-----

Install or ship these files together:

    ttychatter.mailbot
    ttychatter.mailbot.1
    README
    MAILBOT-WALKTHROUGH
    MAILBOT-0.6.0-UPGRADE-NOTES.txt
    MAILBOT-0.6.0-VERIFICATION.txt
    CHANGELOG

Typical local commands
----------------------

    ttychatter.mailbot --doctor
    ttychatter.mailbot --list-candidates
    ttychatter.mailbot --sessions
    ttychatter.mailbot --manpage
    ttychatter.mailbot --model-cache
    ttychatter.mailbot --model-search claude
    ttychatter.mailbot --update-models --dry-run
    ttychatter.mailbot --delete-session old-session --dry-run
    ttychatter.mailbot --cleanup-public-sessions --dry-run
    ttychatter.mailbot --config
    ttychatter.mailbot --raw-config
    ttychatter.mailbot --once --dry-run
    ttychatter.mailbot --once

Private mail subject grammar
----------------------------

Control subjects are bare. Chat-session subjects are braced.

    tc:CONFIG                    Export or apply mailbot config
    tc:MODELS                    Return existing models-cache.json; no refresh
    tc:MODELS TEXT               Search existing model cache for TEXT; no refresh
    tc:CMD                       Run first nonempty body line as a command
    tc:command args              Run a mailbot command directly
    tc:delete{session-name}      Delete a session log
    tc:rm{session-name}          Alias for delete{session-name}
    tc:user                      Show the sender registry/profile status
    tc:prefs                     Show per-user ttychatter preferences
    tc:set MODEL openrouter/free Set an allowed per-user ttychatter option
    tc:set CONTEXT_TURNS 12      Set another allowed per-user option
    tc:api-key KEY               Store this sender's OpenRouter key privately
    tc:forget-api-key            Delete this sender's stored OpenRouter key
    tc:{session-name}            Send body to a ttychatter session
    tc:{new}                     Start a new chat using ttychatter's own default
                                  session-YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM-SS name

Legacy forms remain accepted after ALLOWED_FROM passes:

    tc:{CONFIG}
    tc:{MAILBOT}
    tc:{MAILBOT:command args}

Body-command subjects
---------------------

BODY_COMMAND_SUBJECTS defaults to MAILBOT,CMD.

Examples:

    Subject: MAILBOT

    sessions

or:

    Subject: MAILBOT

    model-search claude

or:

    Subject: MAILBOT

    session worklog
    Explain this attachment.

If the first nonempty body line is a known mailbot command, it runs as a
command. If it is not a known command, the message body is treated as an AI
prompt and routed to a new/default chat session. In public mode, that default
session is still isolated by PUBLIC_SESSION_MODE.

User registry, subject sessions, and API-key secrets
-----------------------------------------------------

USER_REGISTRY=1 is enabled by default. The mailbot maps each accepted sender to
an internal user id and runs ttychatter.python with that sender's private HOME.
This keeps ttychatter config, sessions, caches, and preferences separate between
senders.

SUBJECT_SESSIONS=1 is also enabled by default. After control subjects are
checked, an ordinary subject becomes the session name. Reply prefixes such as
Re: and Fwd: are stripped, so this pair uses the same private session:

    Subject: OpenRouter test
    Subject: Re: OpenRouter test

Per-user preferences are stored in the sender's private ttychatter config. Email
mutation is restricted to USER_TTYCONFIG_ALLOWED_KEYS. Typical commands:

    tc:user
    tc:prefs
    tc:set MODEL openrouter/free
    tc:set CONTEXT_TURNS 12
    tc:unset MODEL

Server fallback OpenRouter key setup:

    install -d -m 700 ~/.secrets/ttychatter/mailbot
    printf '%s\n' 'sk-or-v1-your-server-key' > ~/.secrets/ttychatter/mailbot/openrouter-default.key
    chmod 600 ~/.secrets/ttychatter/mailbot/openrouter-default.key

The mailbot reads that file through DEFAULT_OPENROUTER_API_KEY_FILE and injects
OPENROUTER_API_KEY into the ttychatter subprocess environment. The key is not
written to the normal ttychatter config file.

Users who bring their own key can send:

    tc:api-key sk-or-v1-user-key

or:

    tc:set OPENROUTER_API_KEY sk-or-v1-user-key

The key is immediately written to USER_SECRET_DIR as a chmod 600 secret file.
The user's config only references behavior preferences; the literal key is not
kept there and mailbot replies redact API-key commands.

By default, MIGRATE_PLAINTEXT_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=1 migrates legacy plaintext
OPENROUTER_API_KEY, API_KEY, and TTYCHATTER_API_KEY lines from:

    ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/config

to:

    ~/.secrets/ttychatter/mailbot/openrouter-default.key

A timestamped backup of the old config is left beside the original.

Configurable grammar
--------------------

The default private grammar is tc:{session-name} for sessions and tc:command
for commands. These pieces are configurable:

    SUBJECT_PREFIX=tc
    SUBJECT_SEPARATOR=:
    SESSION_OPEN_DELIM={
    SESSION_CLOSE_DELIM=}
    BODY_COMMAND_SUBJECTS=MAILBOT,CMD
    ALLOW_COMMANDS_IN_SUBJECT=1
    ALLOW_BODY_COMMAND_SUBJECTS=1
    ALLOW_BARE_CONTROL_SUBJECTS=1
    ALLOW_LEGACY_BRACED_CONTROL=1
    RE_SESSION_SUBJECTS=public

The internal command names remain conventional and stable. The grammar overlay
is configurable so a local deployment can match its mail culture without turning
the command parser into a second scripting language.

Public demo mode
----------------

Enable public mode with either:

    ttychatter.mailbot --open-to-public --once

or:

    OPEN_TO_PUBLIC=1

In public mode:

    - any sender may send an ordinary prompt;
    - non-ALLOWED_FROM senders cannot edit config;
    - non-ALLOWED_FROM senders cannot update model caches unless explicitly
      allowed, and update-models should normally stay private;
    - non-ALLOWED_FROM senders cannot delete or rename other sessions;
    - public sessions are isolated by PUBLIC_SESSION_MODE;
    - public replies use Subject: Re: public-session-name;
    - replying to that subject resumes the session when RE_SESSION_SUBJECTS is
      public or always.

Recommended public config:

    OPEN_TO_PUBLIC=1
    USER_REGISTRY=1
    SUBJECT_SESSIONS=1
    REJECT_SELF_SENDER=1
    DENY_FROM=mailbot@thatiam.vip
    SPAM_PROTECTION=1
    RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_MODE=per-sender
    PUBLIC_SESSION_PREFIX=public
    PUBLIC_ALLOW_NAMED_SESSIONS=0
    PUBLIC_ALLOWED_COMMANDS=help,manpage,model-cache,model-search,archive-session
    PUBLIC_SESSION_CLEANUP=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=72
    PUBLIC_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=168
    PUBLIC_SESSION_MAX_COUNT=200
    PUBLIC_SESSION_MAX_TURNS=12
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_ON_DELETE=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_USER_TOGGLE=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_FAILURE_POLICY=keep
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_MAX_BYTES=1048576

Public session cleanup policy
-----------------------------

The default public cleanup policy is a hybrid:

    PUBLIC_SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=72
        Delete sessions that have been inactive for 72 hours.

    PUBLIC_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=168
        Delete sessions older than seven days even if they have occasional
        activity.

    PUBLIC_SESSION_MAX_COUNT=200
        Keep no more than the newest 200 public session logs.

    PUBLIC_SESSION_MAX_TURNS=12
        Trim surviving public logs to the last 12 user/assistant cycles.
        Set to 0 to disable transcript trimming.

This policy favors predictable cleanup over perfect retention. Idle expiry is
the primary rule. TTL and count caps are backstops. Transcript trimming keeps a
demo useful without keeping an indefinitely growing public transcript.

Public archive-on-expire
------------------------

When PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_ON_DELETE=1, cleanup tries to email an expiring
public log to the recorded sender before deleting the local copy.

    PUBLIC_SESSION_INDEX_FILE=~/.local/state/ttychatter/mailbot/public-sessions.json

stores the sender/session mapping needed for that archive mail. The index stores
public sender addresses because the bot cannot email an expiring log without an
address to send it to.

Public users can manage their own preference when enabled:

    tc:archive-session status
    tc:archive-session on
    tc:archive-session off
    tc:archive-opt-in
    tc:archive-opt-out

If archiving is enabled and sending fails, the default is:

    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_FAILURE_POLICY=keep

That means the local log is not deleted unless the archive mail was sent. Set:

    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_FAILURE_POLICY=delete

when disk cleanup must win even if archive delivery fails.

Model cache and model list commands
-----------------------------------

Cache-only commands never ping OpenRouter:

    tc:MODELS
    tc:model-cache
    tc:MODELS claude
    tc:model-search claude

Refresh is explicit:

    tc:update-models

Model-list display through ttychatter.python is separate:

    tc:model-list --all
    tc:models --all
    tc:routers --model-type chat

Output delivery modes
---------------------

Command results can be delivered in the email body, as an attachment, or by an
auto policy.

Global default:

    COMMAND_OUTPUT_MODE=auto

Command-specific overrides:

    MODEL_CACHE_OUTPUT=auto
    MODEL_SEARCH_OUTPUT=body
    MODEL_LIST_OUTPUT=auto
    SESSIONS_OUTPUT=body
    CONFIG_OUTPUT=attachment
    OUTPUT_INLINE_MAX_BYTES=50000

The default auto policy is body for short readable results and attachment for
large or structured results. This means model-search usually returns in the
body, while full config and large model-cache results normally become
attachments.

Session commands
----------------

    tc:sessions
    tc:search TEXT
    tc:search-session SESSION TEXT
    tc:rename-session OLD NEW
    tc:delete-session SESSION
    tc:delete{SESSION}
    tc:rm{SESSION}

Destructive commands support dry-run locally:

    ttychatter.mailbot --delete-session SESSION --dry-run

A dry run shows the target path and does not delete anything. This is useful
because email subject typos are easy and session logs are ordinary files.

Security notes
--------------

User isolation and secret handling are defense layers, not a substitute for a
conservative mail perimeter. Keep ALLOWED_FROM narrow for private deployments.
For public deployments, keep PUBLIC_ALLOWED_COMMANDS small and do not expose
config mutation, model refresh, deletion, rename, api-key, tty-set, or tty-unset
unless the deployment is intentionally disposable.

SPAM_PROTECTION=1 rejects common automated mail classes before any ttychatter
work is attempted:

    - Auto-Submitted headers unless spam_reject_auto_submitted is disabled.
    - List/Bulk/Precedence junk unless spam_reject_list_mail is disabled.
    - Messages with more than SPAM_MAX_RECIPIENTS visible recipients.

RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=1 maintains a small JSON counter database and enforces per
sender and global hourly caps. Public unprivileged senders use stricter caps:

    RATE_LIMIT_SENDER_MESSAGES=20
    RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_MESSAGES=200
    RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_SENDER_MESSAGES=6
    RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_GLOBAL_MESSAGES=60

The mailbot cannot prove SPF, DKIM, DMARC, greylisting, or reputation by itself.
Enforce those at the MTA if public exposure matters.

Self-sender and denylist safety
---------------------------------

Public mode may use:

    ALLOWED_FROM=*

but the bot should still reject itself.  The default is:

    REJECT_SELF_SENDER=1

This check runs before ALLOWED_FROM and before OPEN_TO_PUBLIC.  Therefore the
bot refuses mail from its own MAIL_FROM identity even when ALLOWED_FROM=* is
configured.

A separate denylist is also supported:

    DENY_FROM=mailbot@thatiam.vip,bad@example.org,@bad-domain.example

The standardized key is DENY_FROM.  BANNED_FROM and BANNED_LIST are accepted
as compatibility aliases.  Address entries match exact senders.  @domain and
*@domain entries match all senders at that domain.

Private mode should keep ALLOWED_FROM narrow. Public mode should normally use a
separate mailbox and a small PUBLIC_ALLOWED_COMMANDS list. Do not expose config
mutation, model refresh, session deletion, tty-set, or tty-unset to public
senders unless the deployment is intentionally disposable.

Plaintext IMAP_PASSWORD and SMTP_PASSWORD are redacted from config exports.
Encrypted password file paths are not redacted. OpenBSD cron and daemon runs
should either use a working gpg-agent setup, a protected plaintext local config,
or a host-specific secret mechanism.

0.6.1 abuse-control additions
-----------------------------

0.6.1 keeps the 0.6.0 user registry and adds the missing mail-to-API caps:
MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES, MAX_ATTACHMENT_COUNT, MAX_TOTAL_ATTACHMENT_BYTES,
DAILY_QUOTA_ENABLED, DAILY_QUOTA_DB, DAILY_QUOTA_SENDER_MESSAGES,
DAILY_QUOTA_PUBLIC_SENDER_MESSAGES, DAILY_QUOTA_GLOBAL_MESSAGES,
MAX_SESSIONS_PER_SENDER, API_COST_PROTECTION, API_MAX_INPUT_CHARS,
API_MAX_ESTIMATED_TOKENS, API_MAX_ATTACHMENT_BYTES, and
ABUSE_LIMIT_ALLOWED_FROM.

Use ALLOWED_FROM as the admin/allowlist. By default ABUSE_LIMIT_ALLOWED_FROM=1,
so admins are still capped. Set ABUSE_LIMIT_ALLOWED_FROM=0 only on a private
maintenance mailbox where trusted admins need to bypass message/quota/session/API
caps.
